          "passage": "<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Super-alloys are a group of metal alloys that have two or more characteristics. These include excellent resistance to stress, resistance to corrosion, and resistance to extreme temperatures. Because of these characteristics, super-alloys are commonly used in safety-critical parts where stress and high temperatures could cause failure.</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;When exposed to the high temperatures found in engines, a regular metal alloy might creep - or expand - causing an engine failure. Super-alloys do not creep. One type of super-alloy is stainless steel. While many people believe that stainless steel is used to make cutlery, surgical tools, and buildings, most stainless steel is, in fact, used in safety-critical parts in engines and machines where the stainless steel's characteristics are essential to the safe operation of the machine.</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;The majority of super-alloys are based on nickel, iron, or cobalt. Iron and cobalt super-alloys are quite common, but the most common super-alloys are those containing nickel. A staggering 60 percent of the nickel produced each year is used in making super-alloys.</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Nickel is extracted from ore by roasting at high temperatures. <strong>[A]</strong> This achieves up to a 75 percent purity level, which is enough for the many alloys creating stainless steel. <strong>[B]</strong> For more resistant alloys, however, the nickel must be <strong>refined</strong> further. <strong>[C]</strong> The most common nickel-refining process is a three-step procedure of flotation, smelting, and something called the Sherritt-Gordon process, in which the nickel is treated with hydrogen sulfide (which removes any copper) and then a solvent that separates the nickel from any cobalt. <strong>[D]</strong> <strong>This procedure</strong> can achieve 99 percent purity, which is enough for most industrial applications, but is still not high-purity nickel.</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;High-purity nickel is further <strong>refined</strong> using the Mond process achieving 99.99 percent purity to create high-purity nickel. Named after its creator, Ludwig Mond, this process involves treating the nickel with carbon monoxide to form a gas, and then heating the gas to a very high temperature. At high temperatures, the gas decomposes into its two parts, carbon monoxide and high-purity nickel.</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;High-purity nickel is costly to produce. Only two companies worldwide produce high-purity nickel, and is extremely valuable because only a small portion of the nickel mined worldwide is ever <strong>refined</strong> to the required state of purity to be labeled high-purity nickel.</p>",
